# Terraform state can contain resource metadata and must never be committed;
# the S3 backend is the only source of truth.
.terraform/
# .terraform.lock.hcl IS committed: it pins provider versions/hashes so a
# later apply cannot silently pick up a different provider build.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
*.tfvars
# Terraform also auto-loads the JSON forms; without these an operator's
# environment values could be committed as trackable files.
*.tfvars.json
*.auto.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars.json

# ONE narrow exception, and it is deliberate. The rules above exist to keep an
# operator's local values -- which may hold secrets -- out of the repository.
# app/production.tfvars is the opposite: it is the declared production input
# set, it holds no secret (every value in it is already a `default` in
# variables.tf, and the basic-auth PASSWORD is generated by random_password,
# never written here), and the deploy workflow passes it with -var-file. It has
# to be tracked or every deploy fails on a missing file.
#
# Note it is NOT named *.auto.tfvars: auto-loading would apply it to every
# terraform invocation in this module including a developer's local plan, which
# is exactly the implicitness this file is trying to avoid. It applies only
# where a command names it.
!app/production.tfvars
!example.tfvars
crash.log
